 ### Browser tests (optional, a separate lock)
 `requirements-browser.in` lists one direct pin: `playwright==1.63.0`. Two lock files made with `uv` (the files do not record the uv version; the comment in `requirements-coverage.in` names uv 0.11.32 for the coverage lock, and that the same version made these two was not checked), each with hashes and each held to the pins of the matching development lock (`-c`), so installing it after the development lock changes no development pin: `requirements-browser.txt` (`uv pip compile requirements-browser.in --generate-hashes --python-version 3.12 --python-platform linux --no-strip-extras --exclude-newer 2026-11-12 -c requirements-dev.txt -o requirements-browser.txt`) and `requirements-browser-win.txt` (the same with `--python-version 3.13 --python-platform windows`, `-c requirements-dev-win.txt` and `-o requirements-browser-win.txt`). `--exclude-newer` ignores any release uploaded after that date, so the same command gives the same lock later. Install: `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements-browser.txt` after `requirements-dev.txt`. The checks in `.github/workflows/lint.yml` do not install it (no browser job yet).
 
-Side effect of the review-tool settings: `.codeant/configuration.json` lists both browser lock files (`requirements-browser.txt`, `requirements-browser-win.txt`) among the files its review bot skips, next to the two development locks, because their content is hash lines. So, going by that settings file, the bot's check of dependencies for known vulnerabilities no longer reads these two files; the bot itself was not run or checked here. The settings file does not skip `requirements-browser.in`, which names `playwright==1.63.0`, but that file does not name the packages that only the lock files list (greenlet and pyee; typing-extensions is also listed only in locks, which are skipped).
+Side effect of the review-tool settings: `.codeant/configuration.json` lists both browser lock files (`requirements-browser.txt`, `requirements-browser-win.txt`) among the files its review bot skips, next to the two development locks, because their content is hash lines. So, going by that settings file, the bot's check of dependencies for known vulnerabilities no longer reads these two files; the bot itself was not run or checked here. The settings file does not skip `requirements-browser.in`, which names `playwright==1.63.0`, but that file does not name the packages that only the lock files list (greenlet and pyee; typing-extensions is also listed only in the lock files, which are skipped).
 
 Secret-scan allowance for the browser lock files: the entry in `.gitleaks.toml` accepts a 64-character hash line only in a file named `requirements-browser.txt` or `requirements-browser-win.txt` at the repository root (`paths = ['''^requirements-browser(-win)?\.txt$''']`). The secret scan in `tests/test_hygiene.py` runs from the repository root, so the path is relative and a copy in a sub-folder is not allowed: a planted hash line in a file of that name inside a sub-folder is reported by `gitleaks` (the author tried this once with gitleaks 8.30.1: one finding; no log is shipped and `gitleaks` was not run here), and the second check, the pattern list minus `exceptions.txt`, which is also root-anchored, reports it as well (the test `test_exceptions_narrow` in `tests/test_hygiene.py` plants such a file in a sub-folder and expects it to be reported).
 
